{
  "summary": "This study investigates how dual twisted interfaces in CVD-grown trilayer MoS2 affect second harmonic generation (SHG). By fabricating nine distinct stacking configurations, the researchers analyzed Raman and photoluminescence data to identify stacking-dependent phonon and excitonic responses. Atomic-resolution microscopy confirmed dual twisted interfaces and a supermoiré lattice. The study developed a framework predicting SHG intensity and polarization based on interface geometry, demonstrating TT-MoS2's potential as a tunable nonlinear optical platform.",
  "key_findings": [
    "CVD-grown trilayer MoS2 with dual twisted interfaces exhibits stacking-dependent phonon and excitonic responses",
    "Atomic-resolution microscopy confirmed dual twisted interfaces and a supermoiré lattice structure",
    "A framework linking interface geometry to SHG intensity and polarization was established"
  ],
  "study_type": "Chemical vapor deposition grown trilayer MoS2 with nine distinct stacking configurations",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a foundational framework for engineering multi-interface twisted couplings to tailor nonlinear optical responses in van der Waals materials."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated a nanostructured gradient Ta-O-F coating on 316L stainless steel to address early bacterial infection and poor osseointegration in orthopedic implants. The coating combines a corrosion-stable Ta2O5 barrier with a fluoride reservoir to regulate ion release, improving antibacterial activity against pathogens and promoting osteoblastic and endothelial responses without burst exposure. In vivo testing showed reduced inflammation and bacterial load while enhancing bone regeneration.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The nanostructured gradient Ta-O-F coating suppresses burst fluoride release and maintains antibacterial efficacy against S. aureus and E. coli.",
    "The coating enhances osteoblastic adhesion, proliferation, matrix mineralization, and vascularization in vitro.",
    "In infected rat models, the coating reduced local bacterial burden and inflammation while improving peri-implant bone architecture."
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo studies",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a barrier-reservoir strategy for synchronizing anti-infective and osteoregenerative functions on metallic implants, addressing a critical challenge in orthopedic implant failure."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates why deep subcortical brain regions exhibit greater neuronal diversity than cortical areas despite having fewer neurons. Using whole-brain cell atlases, it proposes a dual subsystem model where the 'learning subsystem' scales through repetitive module replication, while the 'steering subsystem' diversifies specialized cell types for innate behaviors. The findings suggest evolutionary mechanisms underlying brain architecture and potential implications for AI model design.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Deep subcusing regions like the hypothalamus and brainstem show higher neuronal diversity than cortical areas",
    "The learning subsystem (cortex, striatum, cerebellum) scales via repetitive module replication",
    "The steering subsystem (hypothalamus, pallidum, brainstem) diversifies specialized cell types for innate drives"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This framework provides a unified evolutionary explanation for brain architecture divergence between learning and steering subsystems, advancing understanding of how neural diversity supports innate behaviors and potential AI applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This perspective addresses the reproducibility crisis in metal-bacteria interaction research despite its long-standing antibacterial significance. It identifies key sources of inconsistency, proposes a source-specific experimental roadmap, and emphasizes mechanistic investigations requiring causal perturbations, orthogonal measurements, and cross-laboratory validation to improve reproducibility and advance bioengineering applications.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The reproduc: The reproducibility crisis in metal-bacteria studies stems from inconsistent methodologies and lack of mechanistic depth.",
    "A source-specific experimental roadmap is proposed to standardize protocols and enhance reproducibility.",
    "Mechanistic investigations must integrate causal perturbations, orthogonal measurements, and cross-laboratory validation for robust findings."
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a framework for mechanistic investigations in metal-bacteria interactions, addressing reproducibility challenges to strengthen the reliability of antibacterial applications in bioengineering."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how spontaneous phase separation in Janus hydrogels spatiotemporally orchestrates fibrotic progression for functional tendon regeneration. The researchers engineered a Janus hydrogel (SPS-JH) using methacrylated hyaluronic acid and PLD polymers via spontaneous phase separation. The outer HAM layer prevents adhesions, while the inner PLD layer provides mechanical strength. SPS-JH releases COX-2 siRNA early to inhibit hyperactivated fibroblasts and niclosamide later to suppress fibrogenesis, reducing adhesions and promoting functional tendon repair in rats.",
  "key_findings": [
    "SPS-JH reduces peritendinous adhesions in rats with Achilles tendon injury",
    "The hydrogel releases COX-2 siRNA to silence fibroblast hyperactivation during early inflammation",
    "SPS-JH sustains niclosamide release to polarize macrophages and suppress fibrogenesis during proliferation"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a structurally minimalist hydrogel design that uses spontaneous phase separation to coordinate fibrotic progression, enabling functional tendon regeneration without complex fabrication."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates sex-based differences in chronic liver disease (CLD) using metabolomics data from 9,254 individuals. It identifies isoursodeoxycholic acid (isoUDCA) as a bile acid mediating female hepatoprotection. The research demonstrates that estrogen enriches a bacterial consortium producing isoUDCA, which enhances SREBP2 nuclear accumulation, upregulating STARD4 and CYP7B1 to improve cholesterol handling. These findings suggest a microbiome-bile acid axis contributing to sex disparities in CLD susceptibility.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Estrogen selectively enriches a bacterial consortium that produces isoursodeoxycholic acid (isoUDCA)",
    "IsoUDCA promotes SREBP2 nuclear accumulation, upregulating STARD4 and CYP7B1",
    "The microbiome-bile acid axis mediates female-specific protection against chronic liver disease"
  ],
  "study_type": "Metabolomics database analysis",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a mechanistic link between gut microbiota, bile acids, and sex-specific liver disease susceptibility, providing a potential target for sex-tailored therapeutic interventions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of achieving low threshold voltage (Vth) and high performance in n-type organic electrochemical transistors (OECTs). Using a stepwise cyanation strategy on quinoxaline-based polymers, researchers developed QxCN-CNTVT with a deep LUMO level (-4.23 eV). The resulting OECTs achieved a high µC* value of 168.89 F cm-1 V-1 s-1 and a low Vth of 0.13 V, while maintaining stability and enabling efficient signal amplification for electrooculogram and electrocardiogram applications.",
  "key_findings": [
    "QxCN-CNTVT exhibits a deep LUMO level of -4.23 eV",
    "OECTs achieve a high µC* value of 168.89 F cm-1 V-1 s-1 and low Vth of 0.13 V",
    "The material enables efficient amplification of EOG and ECG signals and logic gate operations"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro synthesis and device testing",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a molecular design strategy for n-type OECTs that simultaneously achieves low threshold voltage and high performance, advancing low-power bioelectronic applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of achieving targeted retention for nanomotors by introducing a PNA-triggered engine-disassembly braking mechanism. The approach uses programmable P. nucleic acid to anchor catalytic engines to the carrier, enabling in vivo-controlled disassembly upon target site arrival. Results show enhanced tumor targeting and penetration, with PNA-induced braking leading to optimal tumor suppression in mouse models.",
  "key_findings": [
    "PNA-triggered disassembly of catalytic engines enables precise in vivo braking of nanomotors",
    "The mechanism allows for targeted tumor accumulation and deep tissue penetration",
    "PNA administration after intravenous injection maximizes tumor suppression effects"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo-controlled nanomotor braking mechanism",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a programmable method for nanomotors to achieve active retention at target sites, advancing intelligent drug delivery systems with improved therapeutic efficacy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how lifestyle factors induce regulatory T cell fragility leading to immune-related adverse events (irAEs) during immune checkpoint blockade. Using a mouse model where diet alters gut microbiota and metabolism, researchers recreated human-like irAEs. Key findings include Akkermansia muciniphila enrichment driving Treg instability, sex and age-dependent severity, and interventions like dietary changes or IFN-γ blockade resolving inflammation. The LISA model provides mechanistic insights into irAEs and potential mitigation strategies.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Diet-driven dysbiosis with Akkermansia muciniphila enrichment causes regulatory T cell fragility",
    "Sex and age modulate the severity of immune-related adverse events",
    "Interventions targeting interferon-gamma signaling or diet restore regulatory T cell stability"
  ],
  "study_type": "Mouse model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes dietary and microbial factors as critical determinants of immune checkpoint therapy toxicity, offering a mechanistic framework for reducing adverse events while improving immunotherapy efficacy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the electronic structure of the layered altermagnet CoNb4Se8 using termination-resolved micro-ARPES, spin-ARPES, and DFT. It resolves spin-split branches, demonstrates spin polarization reversal across time-reversal domains, and identifies a weak low-temperature anomaly near 50-6. The findings establish a momentum-resolved benchmark for layered altermagnets.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Direct resolution of spin-split branches in altermagnetic pairs via photoemission spectra",
    "Reversal of spin polarization at opposite momenta and between sample regions, indicating time-reversal domains",
    "Weak low-temperature anomaly near 50-60 K detected through multiple experimental techniques"
  ],
  "study_type": "Termination-resolved micro-ARPES, spin-ARPES, and density functional theory",
  "scientific_significance": "The work provides a momentum-resolved electronic structure benchmark for layered altermagnets, advancing understanding of noncollinear magnetic phases with spin splitting."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses challenges in liver tissue engineering by developing a photocurable hydrogel combining bovine serum albumin methacrylate with decellularized liver extracellular matrix. The hydrogel encapsulated hepatocyte spheroids to create functional engineered hepatic tissue, enhancing viability, polarity, and metabolic repair while reducing inflammation and fibrosis in vivo after implantation in rat liver injury models.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The hydrogel supported hepatocyte expansion and vascularization via VEGF/FGF upregulation and PI3K-AKT pathway activation",
    "It reduced inflammatory responses and promoted M2 macrophage polarization in vivo",
    "Implanted tissue alleviated liver injury, collagen deposition, and fibrosis in CCl4-induced rat models"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro hepatocyte spheroid encapsulation with in vivo rat implantation",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a pro-regenerative microenvironment that integrates immune modulation and vascular induction for scalable liver tissue engineering and regenerative therapy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated metabolic heterogeneity in small cell lung cancer (SCLC) by analyzing transcriptomic and metabolomic data across neuroendocrine (NE) and non-NE cellular states. It identified a universal cysteine dependency and demonstrated that NE cells with low ASCL1 undergo ferroptosis upon cysteine depletion, while ASCL1high cells undergo apoptosis and resist ferroptosis due to GCH1-mediated antioxidant production. Cysteine depletion combined with BH4/BH2 inhibition reduced tumor growth in xenografts.",
  "key_findings": [
    "SCLC cells exhibit universal dependency on exogenous cysteine across neuroendocrine and non-neuroendocrine states",
    "ASCL1low neuroendocrine SCLC cells undergo ferroptosis when cysteine is depleted",
    "ASCL1high SCLC cells resist ferroptosis via GCH1-driven BH4/BH2 synthesis"
  ],
  "study_type": "Integrated transcriptomic and metabolomic analyses",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings establish metabolic state as a determinant of cell death susceptibility in SCLC, providing a mechanistic basis for targeted therapies exploiting cysteine-dependent ferroptosis resistance."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates closed nanopores in silicon-carbon anodes as stress-regulation units to mitigate lithiation-induced deformation and interphase degradation. By engineering heteroatom-doped carbon hosts with abundant closed pores, the material redirects expansion stress inward, suppresses electrode swelling, and enhances Li+ kinetics. The nitrogen-doped composite achieves 1756.5 mAh g-1 reversible capacity, 90.2% initial Coulombic efficiency, and 868.4 mAh g-1 at 3 C, with reduced expansion from 38.4% to 13.1% after 50 cycles. The approach demonstrates scalability for practical battery applications.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Closed nanopores in heteroatom-doped carbon hosts redirect expansion stress inward, suppressing electrode swelling",
    "Nitrogen-doped silicon-carbon composites achieve 1756.5 mAh g-1 reversible capacity and 868.4 mAh g-1 at 3 C",
    "The design reduces electrode thickness expansion from 38.4% to 13.1% after 50 cycles and enables scalable production"
  ],
  "study_type": "Material design and synthesis",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes closed-pore stress regulation as a scalable structural strategy for practical low-expansion silicon-carbon anodes, addressing critical mechanical and electrochemical instability challenges in high-energy lithium-ion batteries."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated whether piezoelectric electroporation could enhance high-frequency irreversible electroporation (H-FIRE) for prostate cancer eradication. Researchers identified DNA damage repair as the mechanism allowing residual tumor cell survival post-H-FIRE. They engineered a piezoelectric nanomaterial (BOIS) that, when activated by ultrasound, generates electric fields and reactive oxygen species to convert reversibly injured cells into irreversible death. The approach leverages H-FIRE as a delivery mechanism for BOIS, creating a self-reinforcing loop that significantly improves residual tumor cell elimination.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Piezoelectric nanomaterial BOIS delivered via H-FIRE enhances residual tumor cell destruction",
    "BOIS activation by ultrasound produces electric fields and ROS to induce irreversible cell death",
    "This strategy converts reversibly injured tumor cells into irreversible lethal damage through a self-reinforcing therapeutic loop"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a novel therapeutic paradigm where focal ablation serves as a delivery mechanism for nanomaterials, enabling self-reinforcing elimination of residual tumor cells post-therapy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates taurine's role in mitigating inflammaging by regulating the NLRP3 inflammasome in macrophages. Aging increases circulating taurine in humans and mice, and elevated taurine in aged mice improves health span, reduces sepsis mortality, and suppresses inflammasome-mediated inflammation. Mechanistically, taurine efflux from macrophages upon NLRP3 activation prevents inflammasome activation, suggesting taurine acts as an immunometabolic rheostat to balance immune responses.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Aging increases circulating taurine concentrations in humans and mice",
    "Elevated taurine in aged mice enhances health span and reduces mortality from sepsis and inflammasome-mediated inflammation",
    "Taurine efflux from macrophages prevents NLRP3 inflammasome activation by inhibiting trans-Golgi network disassembly"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies taurine as a critical immunometabolic regulator that suppresses NLRP3 inflammasome activation, offering a potential therapeutic target for inflammaging and inflammasomopathies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates decoupling biomass electrooxidation from oxygen evolution reaction (OER) using e-waste-derived gold electrocatalysts. The researchers developed a nanostructured Au catalyst via corrosion-template-assisted electrodeposition from electronic waste. The catalyst enables low-voltage hydrogen production and scalable 5-hydroxymethylfurfural conversion to 2-furoic acid at reduced cell voltages, demonstrating efficient tandem electrochemical upgrading with sustained current densities over 200 hours.",
  "key_findings": [
    "E-waste-derived Au electrocatalysts enable low-voltage hydrogen production via decoupled biomass oxidation",
    "The catalyst achieves selective aldehyde oxidation to 2-furoic acid at potentials below OER requirements",
    "Sustained current densities of 0.6 A cm-2 at 1.2 V for over 200 hours in flow-electrolysis"
  ],
  "study_type": "Electrochemical",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a scalable electrochemical platform that reduces energy input for biomass conversion while simultaneously enabling low-voltage hydrogen production through strategic decoupling of oxidation pathways."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates targeted KRAS gene correction in non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) using transcription activator-like effector (TALE) base editing delivered via cancer-targeted lipid nanoparticles. The approach combines TALE-guided C-to-T base editing to introduce stop codons in KRAS, with lipid nanoparticles engineered using EGFR peptides for tumor-specific delivery. In NSCLC mouse models, the targeted TALE LNP demonstrated significant tumor suppression by disrupting KRAS oncogenic signaling.",
  "key_findings": [
    "TALE-guided C-to-T base editing creates stop codons in KRAS oncogene",
    "Targeted lipid nanoparticles with EGFR peptide achieve tumor-specific delivery",
    "NSCLC mouse models show reduced tumor growth via KRAS inactivation"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo mouse model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a targeted therapeutic strategy for KRAS-mutant lung cancer by directly correcting oncogenic drivers using precise genome editing delivered via tumor-homing nanoparticles."
}

{
  "summary": "This study evaluates in vivo genome editing using AAV9 vectors for Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) therapy. Preclinical models demonstrated targeted dystrophin restoration and improved muscle function. Early clinical trials showed detectable dystrophin expression but also a fatal adverse event at higher doses, emphasizing immunologic risks and the narrow therapeutic window for AAV-CRISPR therapies.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Systemic AAV9 delivery achieved targeted genome editing in humanized DMD mice",
    "Non-human primates exhibited efficient on-target editing without dose-limiting toxicity",
    "First-in-human trial revealed detectable dystrophin expression but a fatal adverse event at higher doses"
  ],
  "study_type": "Open-label, dose-escalation clinical trial with preclinical models",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings establish initial human proof-of-concept for muscle genome editing while highlighting critical immunologic risks and therapeutic constraints in AAV-CRISPR therapies for DMD."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of direct tomographic volumetric printing (TVP) of collagen by introducing vitamin C as a redox regulator. It suppresses dityrosine crosslinking in a Ru(II)bpy3/SPS system, establishing a photo-redox threshold that delays crosslinking until vitamin C depletion. This enables high-fidelity collagen printing with improved feature resolution and mechanically stable hydrogels supporting cell viability and high-density seeding.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Vitamin C suppresses Ru(:III) accumulation and scavenges persulfate radicals in the Ru/SPS system",
    "The photo-redox threshold delays dityrosine crosslinking until vitamin C depletion",
    "Collagen prints achieve 80 µm positive and 120 µm negative feature resolution with 180% stretchability"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a biocompatible redox mechanism for precise collagen 3D printing, advancing biofabrication techniques for functional tissue engineering without compromising native protein bioactivity."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how biomimetic hierarchical scaffolds act as spatial bridges to enhance mesenchymal stem cell (MSC) and Schwann cell (SC) interactions for bone regeneration. Using a rabbit bone defect model, the scaffolds improved bone regeneration by 3.24-fold and neuro-vascular network reconstruction by 2.29-fold compared to traditional scaffolds. The findings highlight cytokine-mediated cross-talk and neuro-osteogenic signaling pathways.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Hierarchical multi-channel scaffolds increased bone regeneration by 3.24-fold in rabbit models",
    "Scaffolds enhanced neuro-vascular network reconstruction by 2.29-fold",
    "Cytokine-cytokine receptor interactions activated neurogenesis and osteogenesis pathways"
  ],
  "study_type": "Rabbit bone defect model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes biomimetic hierarchical scaffolds as functional 'spatial bridges' to accelerate multicellular interactions in tissue regeneration, advancing strategies for complex tissue repair through engineered cellular crosstalk."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether symbionts in animal-microbe symbioses remain immobile after losing motile structures. Using long-term tracking, fluorescence, and electron microscopy, researchers observed dinoflagellate symbionts (Amphidinium sp.) moving at 1 μm/s across an acoel worm host (Waminoa sp.) via host actin machinery within ultra-thin parenchymal cells. The mobility is regulated by diel rhythms and regeneration, demonstrating host-driven symbiont transport mechanisms critical for holobiont homeostasis.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Dinoflagellate symbionts move at 1 μm/s across the acoel worm host body",
    "Mobility is driven by host actin machinery within ultra-thin parenchymal cells",
    "Symbiont distribution is spatiotemporally regulated by diel rhythms and regeneration"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work reveals a host-cytoskeleton-driven mechanism for long-range symbiont transport, expanding understanding of how holobionts maintain dynamic homeostasis through active symbiont mobility."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines supercapacitor performance under nonequilibrium conditions like high-frequency cycling and wide temperature ranges. It identifies three degradation mechanisms: transport mismatch, interfacial instability, and thermo-mechanical coupling. The study derives design principles for ion transport, interfacial chemistry, electrolyte optimization, and structural adaptability to enhance stability in dynamic energy storage applications.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Three degradation mechanisms under nonequilibrium conditions: transport mismatch, interfacial instability, and thermo-mechanical coupling",
    "Design principles for ion transport regulation and interfacial chemistry optimization",
    "Strategies to improve supercapacitor stability in high-frequency cycling and wide temperature applications"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a unified mechanistic framework for understanding supercapacitor degradation under dynamic operational conditions, advancing the design of robust energy storage systems for real-world grid applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated whether bio-orthogonally functionalized biomimetic nanoreactors could suppress lung cancer stem-like cells (LCSCs) by disrupting ER-mitochondria crosstalk. Using an N-cadherin-targeting aptamer and photosensitizer bortez. The nanoreactor induced a self-amplifying ER-mitochondria stress loop via ROS generation and proteasome inhibition, resulting in LCSC death through enhanced oxidative damage and reduced stemness markers.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Induced a self-amplifying ER-mitochondria stress loop via ROS and proteasome inhibition",
    "Reduced STAT3 phosphorylation and stemness markers in LCSCs",
    "Enhanced cytotoxicity specifically in lung cancer stem-like cells"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro nanoreactor-based intervention",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a targeted mechanism to eliminate therapy-resistant cancer stem cells through engineered stress feedback loops, advancing strategies for precision cancer therapy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether somatic CAG-repeat expansions in Huntington's disease neurons follow similar pathological pathways in cortical and striatal regions. Using molecular profiling and CAG-repeat analysis, researchers found both neuron types initiate toxicity at ∼150 CAGs but exhibit distinct gene expression changes. Somatic expansions varied up to 20-fold among cortical pyramidal neuron subtypes, with nuclear inclusions forming in neurons exceeding 150 CAGs.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Cortical pyramidal neurons initiate toxicity at ∼150 CAGs, analogous to striatal neurons",
    "Somatic CAG-repeat expansions varied up to 20-fold among cortical pyramidal neuron subtypes",
    "Nuclear inclusions of aggregated HTT protein formed in neurons with CAG expansions >150"
  ],
  "study_type": "Molecular profiling and CAG-repeat analysis",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings demonstrate that somatic CAG-repeat expansions initiate comparable pathological phases in distinct brain regions of Huntington's disease, revealing shared mechanisms despite regional differences in gene expression."
}
